.TH ENCOPT 1 "2026-11-10" "encopt 1.1" "pdp64 tools"
.SH NAME
encopt \- optimal choice of the instruction forms of a prefix-coded encoding
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B encopt
[\fB\-O\fR \fIobjective\fR] [\fB\-k\fR \fIbytes\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIoutput\fR] [\fB\-t\fR \fIseconds\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fInodes\fR] [\fB\-r\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-x\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-C\fR] [\fB\-q\fR] [\fB\-v\fR] \fIinput\fR
.br
.B encopt \-\-version | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
The pdp64 encodes an instruction as a variable number of 16-bit parcels; the length and the form of the instruction are given by a prefix of the first parcel
(a prefix code).  A form whose operands need \fIb\fR bits in an instruction of \fIn\fR parcels takes the prefix length 16n \- b and with it 2^(16\-L) of the 65,536
first-parcel patterns.  By Kraft's inequality a prefix-free assignment exists exactly when the shares add up to at most all of the patterns.
The forms the instruction set cannot do without (the \fIforced\fR forms) are always present; the \fIcandidate\fR forms each shorten some instructions of the corpus and
each costs code space.
.B encopt
chooses the candidates that minimise the objective within the code space that the forced forms leave, and assigns the prefixes (the canonical assignment: shortest prefix first,
each aligned to its size).
.PP
The code space is a parameter of the input (the \fBbudget\fR line).  \fBexport.py\fR writes 65,536, all of the first-parcel patterns: \fIno reserve\fR, the free-space policy is zero.
The candidates may then use what the forced forms leave (65,536 minus the patterns of the forced forms; the patterns of the sub-opcodes that a form leaves unassigned stay inside
its leaf and are illegal instructions).  The study kept 5% free (62,259); \fBexport.py --budget 0.95\fR writes that.
.PP
Three objectives (what is minimised; the weights are the static counts of the corpus, or the dynamic counts of a profile, see \fBencopt\fR(5)):
.TP
.B static
the bytes of the corpus: the sum over the instruction classes of count times the size of the shortest form that encodes it (the default).
.TP
.B fetch
the bytes fetched when the programs run: the same sum with the dynamic (executed) counts.  An instruction that the profile never executes weighs nothing.
.TP
.B exec
the operations executed: each executed instruction costs \fIk\fR bytes-equivalents per operation (an instruction is one operation; a compare-and-branch that no form reaches is two,
\fBcmp\fR and \fBbCC\fR; an instruction that no form encodes counts 99) plus its bytes: cost = executions * (k * operations + bytes), with \fIk\fR = 4096 by default.
The operations come first and the bytes fetched break ties (k is above the size of any instruction, so one operation saved outweighs any change in size of the same execution).
Which forms exist changes the number of operations only through those two cases, so this objective is the operations executed as far as the encoding can influence them
and otherwise the bytes fetched.
.PP
The search is exact (see ALGORITHM) and is reported with its proof status.  The result is deterministic: the same input gives the same bytes of output.
.PP
\fIinput\fR is a file in the format of
.BR encopt (5)
(\fB\-\fR reads standard input), written by
.B export.py
from the data of the Python study in the pdp64 repository (see
.BR encopt-tools (1)),
and later by the compiler's corpus tool.  The result is written in the output format of
.BR encopt (5).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-O " objective" ", \-\-objective " objective
\fBstatic\fR (default), \fBfetch\fR or \fBexec\fR.  The last two need the dynamic counts of a profile in the input (\fBexport.py \-\-weights\fR); without them the program refuses.
.TP
.BI \-k " bytes" ", \-\-opcost " bytes
\fBexec\fR: the bytes that one executed operation counts for (16 to 1,000,000; default 4096).
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the result to \fIfile\fR (default standard output).
.TP
.BI \-t " seconds"
Time limit of the exact search, default 600, 0 for none.  When the limit stops the search the status is \fBbest-found\fR.
.TP
.BI \-n " nodes"
Limit of the branch-and-bound nodes of the search (default none).
.TP
.BI \-r " name"
The candidate \fIname\fR must be chosen (a constraint, repeatable).  The status of a proved result is then \fBoptimal-within-constraint-set\fR.
.TP
.BI \-x " name"
The candidate \fIname\fR may not be chosen (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-C
Do not search: evaluate the \fBcommitted\fR selection of the input (the forms of the grammar in force) and write its prefix assignment, in the order of the
committed lines.  Used to check the assignment against the grammar.
.TP
.B \-q
Nothing on standard error.
.TP
.B \-v
More progress on standard error (twice: every component).
.SH ALGORITHM
The unit of choice is a candidate: one form with one aligned group of mnemonics.  An instruction class of the corpus (a \fIdemand\fR: weight, the bytes the forced forms give
it, the candidates that would give it fewer) is served by the shortest chosen candidate that covers it.  Minimising the weighted bytes under the code-space budget is a
budgeted facility-location problem (NP-hard).  \fBencopt\fR solves it exactly:
.IP 1. 3
Reductions that cannot lose the optimum: candidates dearer than the whole budget are dropped; a candidate that covers a subset of the demands of another that is not
longer and not dearer is dropped (bit-set comparison); the candidates and demands fall apart into independent components (union-find).
.IP 2.
Per component, branch and bound for a given budget with a Lagrangian bound: the budget row is dualised with a multiplier and the remaining unconstrained problem
is bounded by Erlenkotter's dual ascent; the multiplier is searched for each node.
.IP 3.
Per component, the efficient frontier (cost, value) is computed exactly by solving for the largest budget, then for one less than the cost found, and so on, stopping
as soon as the incumbent meets the previous value (a known lower bound).
.IP 4.
Across components, an exact multiple-choice knapsack (dynamic programme over the budget in units of one pattern).
.IP 5.
Only the part of each frontier that can belong to a solution not worse than a threshold is computed: the hulls of the dual-ascent bounds give, for each component and
budget, a lower bound on the best total; the threshold starts at the root bound and grows until the dynamic programme finds a solution below it, which is then optimal.
.PP
The prefix assignment itself is exact and constructive (Kraft); unconstrained Huffman, Hu\-Tucker or package-merge do not apply, because the prefix length of a form
is fixed by its operand bits, not chosen from frequencies.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (including \fBbest-found\fR), 1 when the forced forms alone exceed the budget or the constraints are infeasible, 2 for usage and input errors.
.SH FILES
.I tools/encopt/export.py
writes the input from the Python study, with the corrected reader of the corpus
.RI ( corpusfix.py )
and the weights of a profile;
.I tools/encopt/profile.py
collects the profile;
.I tools/encopt/check.sh
tests.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR encopt (5),
.BR encopt-tools (1),
.I docs/encoding-work/optimizer.md
